{"connection_id": "BC-50e3a6412d1d96410f4a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.25.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dross removed, silver remains", "note": "The commentary links foam from fire to what is discarded while useful material remains; Proverbs says removing dross leaves workable silver, a close refining image for that distinction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7897007541559f60f32", "ref": "WLC:Prov.25.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Dross removed so silver becomes usable", "note": "Removing dross from silver leaves material for the smith, a precise counterpart to the commentary's furnace foam: what is waste departs while what is useful remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-49797dc4194a879a6788", "ref": "WLC:Prov.6.23", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Command as lamp and teaching as light on life's way", "note": "The commandment is a lamp, teaching a light, and reproofs a way of life; it joins reminder, guidance, and life in terms specific to the commentary's movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4accca49c36e852ce4f3", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree by water", "note": "The commentary sets withering pasture against lasting good; Psalm 1's person who delights in instruction is a watered tree whose leaf does not wither, an enduring fruitful counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e6f452e73fd8be2bdda", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Fruitful tree by streams that does not wither", "note": "The water-planted tree bears fruit and does not wither, concretely supplies the commentary's useful, enduring side of water and vegetation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-88620b230fec1a1daf7f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chaff blown away", "note": "The commentary's dead pasture is carried off by flood; Psalm 1 says the wicked are like chaff driven by wind, using agricultural refuse for a contrasting fate."}
{"connection_id": "BC-003be0ee86469437d01c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Chaff driven away", "note": "The wicked are wind-driven chaff, a biblical residue image beside the commentary's flood-carried foam and dried pasture that do not remain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d63c1932578abbdb27d9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.16", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass and passing wind", "note": "The commentary makes the withering pasture an image for worldly life; the psalm compares human days to grass and says wind passes over it, a close mortality image without the commentary's two-field choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b15fecfe75a7b857bc9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.17", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "enduring mercy", "note": "The commentary says the hereafter is better and more abiding than the world; the next psalm verse says the Lord's steadfast love lasts from everlasting to everlasting, supplying a divine permanence beside the grass image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18e4b9717406b00ea768", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "streams in the valleys", "note": "The commentary pictures water running through valleys and sustaining pasture; the psalm describes springs giving drink to wild animals among the mountains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7dbd80f2379cc60911b4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass and useful produce", "note": "The commentary contrasts pasture with refuse and useful remainder; the psalm says God makes grass for cattle and plants for human use, specifying what the land's growth serves."}
{"connection_id": "BC-965aa6d95ab7855b80b8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God causes grass and cultivated vegetation to grow", "note": "The psalm distinguishes grass for animals and vegetation for human cultivation, furnishing a close biblical landscape for the commentary's pasture and worked soil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce53da03d42cca8a0825", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.105", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Word as lamp for foot and light for path", "note": "The word gives a lamp and path-light, supplying a biblical verbal-light counterpart to the commentary's fire sought as guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e89b4cf793529894504", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "woven in the womb", "note": "The commentary joins craft and bodily formation; the psalm says God formed and knit the speaker in the mother's womb, making that joining explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa2d29281d259e6f989a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.15", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "body fashioned in hidden depths", "note": "The commentary links a formed body with what is hidden; the psalm says its frame was not hidden while it was made in secret, joining bodily craft to hiddenness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f18e416d22bdf6a6e49c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.148.13", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The exalted name alone praised", "note": "The psalm calls for praise of YHWH's name because that name alone is exalted above earth and heaven, closely meeting the commentary's name, praise, and highness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8166f1b5dd6beac9dd3a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked like grass", "note": "The commentary says worldly life is a pasture that withers and is swept away; the psalm tells the faithful not to fret because evildoers quickly fade like grass, directly applying the plant image to moral outcomes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa69248c422b80a98ce4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.38", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "peaceful endures", "note": "The commentary contrasts the short-lived world with the abiding hereafter; the psalm asks the reader to mark the blameless person because his future is peace, a related lasting outcome in an ethical contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4448efa0dd8fd9bb2027", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watered furrows and blessed growth", "note": "The commentary pictures measured water carrying foam through valleys before useful growth remains; the psalm says God waters furrows and blesses the crop, a related but non-identical account of controlled water and yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-478f9154cdb77cbad680", "ref": "WLC:Ps.7.18", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praise of YHWH Most High", "note": "The commentary opens by joining praise of the Lord's name to exalted height; this psalm closes its prayer by singing to YHWH's name as Most High, giving a close liturgical counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b7b57ab0bb40b18fba0", "ref": "WLC:Ps.7.18", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ʿElyon and al-Aʿlā", "note": "The commentary's al-Aʿlā and this verse's Hebrew ʿElyon are cognate Semitic forms for “Most High”; the psalm uses the title while praising YHWH's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e6d9aca2af21a548836", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers by evening", "note": "The commentary treats withering pasture as the passing worldly life; the psalm says grass flourishes in the morning and fades by evening, an explicit human-time image without the commentary's later-life contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4922da23b6b443b52277", "ref": "WLC:Ps.92.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous planted in God's house", "note": "The commentary opposes drying pasture to the cultivated field that keeps producing; the psalm pictures the righteous planted in God's courts, grounding the contrasting growth in worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a35704a41e9b8812c4c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.92.15", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruit in old age", "note": "The commentary says lasting good grows and leaves a remainder; the psalm says the righteous still bear fruit in old age, a pointed image of yield that persists beyond ordinary seasons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30b5b3102fe46204ba3c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.92.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked flourish like grass", "note": "The commentary says the preferred worldly life is the withering pasture; the psalm says the wicked sprout like grass only to be destroyed, while the righteous are later pictured as flourishing trees."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab3e3c188725d229331b", "ref": "WLC:Zech.13.9", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A remnant refined by fire and calling God's name", "note": "God brings a remnant through fire, refines it, and answers when it calls the divine name, closely coupling fire, purification, name, and responsive relationship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ac786b08f93c7bd5bdc", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 11:20", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all things by measure", "note": "The commentary links measured water and the creator's measuring; Wisdom says God ordered all things by measure, number, and weight, extending that ordering to creation as a whole."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a0f2dfd09a857dc6294", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 11:20", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "All things arranged by measure, number, and weight", "note": "This Jewish wisdom formula directly parallels the commentary's repeated movement from measured water to a created order apportioned by God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db871c779ab8bfe2e70a", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 17:7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Egyptian magic publicly disgraced", "note": "Wisdom presents the Egyptian magic arts as shamed during the plague darkness, a Jewish expansion of the Moses-versus-magicians setting that makes magical competence fail before God's act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ed6ff2bdc1925e341d41", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:1", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The righteous beyond torment", "note": "The righteous are in God's hand and no torment touches them, providing a Jewish wisdom contrast to the commentary's person destined for the great fire."}
